{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>For</b> — Only great good I did for you, in that I brought you up and brought you out by the hand of a man with whom you can speak and tell him all your needs, and who will make known to you My ways, and they are: <b>Moses</b> — who came to Pharaoh; <b>and Aaron</b> — who prophesied to Israel before Moses came; <b>and Miriam</b> — who prophesied to the women. Also the Torah was given through the hand of Moses and Aaron, who served as his mouth, and both of them taught the commandments to the men and Miriam to the women, <b>with whom the word of HaShem was face to face</b>, as a man speaks to his fellow mouth to mouth, and not in a dream, in a vision, and not in riddles — and this is a great and wondrous good, for the matters are as clear as a father to his son and a teacher to his student."], :book :micah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The verse phrase 'אשר דבר השם עמו פנים אל פנים' is embedded mid-sentence in Ibn Ezra's prose; I set it in bold as a lemma head though it runs syntactically into his argument. The singular suffix in 'עמו' (with him) refers to Moses."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Micah -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/daat/olam_hatanah/mefaresh.asp?book=20&perek=1&mefaresh=ezra"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:14:06.591624981Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}